            "Adolescence — ten to nineteen years — is the second period of rapid growth and brain development, the time when identity, independence and relationships are formed, and when most of the behaviours that determine adult health begin. <strong>Puberty</strong> begins in girls at 8–13 years (breast buds first, then pubic hair, the growth spurt, and menarche about two years after breast buds, at a median of 12–13 years in Ghana) and in boys at 9–14 (testicular enlargement first, then pubic hair, penile growth, voice change and the growth spurt later than girls); the nurse answers questions about wet dreams, breast asymmetry, acne, body odour, menstrual pain and irregular early cycles as normal, recognises <strong>delayed puberty</strong> (no breast development by 13, no menarche by 15–16, no testicular growth by 14 — sickle cell disease, malnutrition, chronic illness, Turner syndrome) and precocious puberty (before 8 in girls, 9 in boys) for referral, and supports <strong>menstrual health</strong> — pads, pain relief, school attendance, and the heavy or painful periods that need treatment.",
            "<strong>Sexual and reproductive health</strong>: about one Ghanaian girl in seven has a child before eighteen, and adolescent pregnancy carries the highest maternal and newborn mortality, the end of schooling and the risk of unsafe abortion. Adolescents have a right to information and to <strong>confidential services</strong> under Ghana's adolescent health policy; the nurse provides, without a parent's permission, counselling, <strong>condoms, emergency contraception, the pill, injectables and long-acting methods</strong> to the sexually active adolescent, STI screening and treatment, HIV testing, HPV vaccination at nine, and pregnancy testing and antenatal care without judgement — while recognising the signs of <strong>sexual abuse and exploitation</strong> (a partner much older, 'sugar daddies' and transactional sex, coercion, a child under sixteen) and the duty to protect (Lesson 1.2). <strong>Mental health</strong>: depression, anxiety and self-harm rise sharply in adolescence and are missed behind 'bad behaviour', headaches and abdominal pain; ask directly about mood, sleep, hopelessness and thoughts of self-harm — asking does not plant the idea — and refer; suicide attempts with organophosphates, paracetamol and chloroquine are emergencies first and mental health referrals second. <strong>Substance use</strong>: alcohol, cigarettes and shisha, cannabis, <strong>tramadol</strong> and codeine syrups, energy drinks, and the glue and petrol sniffed by street children — asked about privately and answered without lecture. <strong>Nutrition</strong>: anaemia in half of adolescent girls (weekly iron–folic acid through the school programme, deworming, nutrition education), under-nutrition, and the rising obesity of the cities. <strong>Injury and violence</strong>: road traffic, fighting, and intimate-partner violence that begins in adolescence.",
